### name: num.unicode-digits-are-not-numbers
--- note
A number is ASCII digits and nothing else (§4). Several hosts have a
Unicode-aware notion of "digit" close to hand — SBCL's DIGIT-CHAR-P and PCRE's
UCP-enabled \d both accept U+0661 ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT ONE — and reaching for it
makes this parse as 1. The differential fuzzer caught exactly that.
--- source
"\u{0661}" + 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM
===
### name: num.unicode-digits-are-not-numeric
--- source
ISNUM("\u{0661}")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: num.unicode-digits-in-hex
--- note
The same trap in FROM_HEX, whose digits are also ASCII-only.
--- source
FROM_HEX("0\u{0661}")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG
===
### name: num.round.scale-beyond-the-cap
--- note
spec/SPEC.md §6.4. Before this cap, the four hosts failed four different ways:
a host RangeError in JS, memory exhaustion in PHP and Lisp, and — after a
32-bit cast — a confident wrong answer in C++.
--- source
ROUND(1.5, 4294967296)
--- expect
error E_RANGE
===
### name: num.power.exponent-beyond-the-cap
--- note
POWER(10, 4294967299) returned 1000 in JS, because the decimal core halved the
exponent with `>>=`, which truncates to 32 bits. The exponent is a count, and
counts here are 64-bit.
--- source
POWER(10, 4294967299)
--- expect
error E_RANGE
===
### name: num.power.exponent-above-32-bits-is-exact
--- note
The guard is a documented cap, not an accident of integer width: an exponent
just inside 32-bit range must still compute exactly rather than wrapping.
--- source
POWER(10, 40)
--- expect
num 10000000000000000000000000000000000000000
===
